00:45:54.090 --> 00:45:55.410
And yet we're in a culture

1054
00:45:55.410 --> 00:45:58.350
where we're increasingly judging everybody

1055
00:45:58.350 --> 00:46:01.890
based on their worst day and canceling people,

1056
00:46:01.890 --> 00:46:05.100
and saying, you go away now and that's it for you.

1057
00:46:05.100 --> 00:46:09.210
There is no room, at least in the social media space,

1058
00:46:09.210 --> 00:46:11.793
for that forgiveness.

1059
00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:15.210
Unless we find a way back to that,

1060
00:46:15.210 --> 00:46:16.972
a way to prioritize it

1061
00:46:16.972 --> 00:46:21.972
and inject it into our kind of discourse and conduct,

1062
00:46:23.760 --> 00:46:24.780
we're lost.

1063
00:46:24.780 --> 00:46:25.710
You are so right.

1064
00:46:25.710 --> 00:46:27.870
I think you put it so well,

1065
00:46:27.870 --> 00:46:30.060
we're judging everybody according to their worst day

1066
00:46:30.060 --> 00:46:32.100
or their most stupid tweet.

1067
00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:33.930
Everything is, I gotcha.

1068
00:46:33.930 --> 00:46:35.610
It's terrible.

1069
00:46:35.610 --> 00:46:39.660
Many years ago, my best friend who has since passed,

1070
00:46:39.660 --> 00:46:43.350
we were sitting in my bedroom and I was on the phone

1071
00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:45.840
with someone who worked for me

1072
00:46:45.840 --> 00:46:48.420
and I started yelling at the guy.

1073
00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:51.027
I got off the phone and Richard said to me, "What was that?"

1074
00:46:51.027 --> 00:46:53.550
And I said, Well, he did this or he didn't do that,

1075
00:46:53.550 --> 00:46:54.660
or whatever it was.

1076
00:46:54.660 --> 00:46:55.657
And Richard looked at me and he went,

1077
00:46:55.657 --> 00:46:58.473
"Marianne, he made a mistake.

1078
00:47:00.660 --> 00:47:02.277
People make mistakes."

1079
00:47:07.245 --> 00:47:08.610
It was a brick to my forehead, I remember.

1080
00:47:08.610 --> 00:47:11.100
And no matter what he did,

1081
00:47:11.100 --> 00:47:12.540
the fact that you were such a bitch about it

1082
00:47:12.540 --> 00:47:13.790
makes you no better than-

1083
00:47:14.700 --> 00:47:15.533
I got it.

1084
00:47:15.533 --> 00:47:18.210
I mean, I'm not a hundred percent practicing it,

1085
00:47:18.210 --> 00:47:19.680
but a lot better than I used to be

1086
00:47:19.680 --> 00:47:22.950
because people make mistakes.

1087
00:47:22.950 --> 00:47:27.950
Yeah, so how do we try to create space for that?

1088
00:47:29.100 --> 00:47:30.420
The Course Of Miracle says, you say,

1089
00:47:30.420 --> 00:47:33.985
Dear God, I'm willing to see this differently.

1090
00:47:33.985 --> 00:47:38.985
The path that we are here to monitor is not somebody else's.

1091
00:47:39.780 --> 00:47:40.613
It's like you were talking about,

1092
00:47:40.613 --> 00:47:41.580
hey, hey, you're not here

1093
00:47:41.580 --> 00:47:42.900
to take somebody else's inventory.

1094
00:47:42.900 --> 00:47:43.950
Right.
You the path,

1095
00:47:43.950 --> 00:47:46.950
you're here to monitor very vigilantly as your own.

1096
00:47:46.950 --> 00:47:49.440
Dear God, I see how self-righteous I am in this moment.

1097
00:47:49.440 --> 00:47:51.240
Dear God, I see how judgmental I am in this moment.

1098
00:47:51.240 --> 00:47:52.860
Dear God, I see how blaming,

1099
00:47:52.860 --> 00:47:54.450
like how do I think I am.

1100
00:47:54.450 --> 00:47:56.490
Dear God, take this away from me.

1101
00:47:56.490 --> 00:48:00.060
And then, and I see this all the time,

1102
00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:03.630
then what happens is sometimes you realize,

1103
00:48:03.630 --> 00:48:04.530
just keep your mouth shut,

1104
00:48:04.530 --> 00:48:06.750
there's nothing that even needs to be said.

1105
00:48:06.750 --> 00:48:08.970
Sometimes you realize, oh, there's something

1106
00:48:08.970 --> 00:48:11.550
that's gonna need to be communicated here,

1107
00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:14.350
but you will communicate it from such a different place.

1108
00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:17.280
You will communicate it from elegance,

1109
00:48:17.280 --> 00:48:18.843
and charity and compassion.

1110
00:48:21.128 --> 00:48:21.961
The Course Of Miracle says,

1111
00:48:21.961 --> 00:48:23.610
"If you're judging and attacking someone,

1112
00:48:23.610 --> 00:48:25.360
you're wrong even if you're right."

1113
00:48:26.520 --> 00:48:29.880
Right, but we have a social media ecosystem.

1114
00:48:29.880 --> 00:48:30.933
Oh yeah, we do.

1115
00:48:30.933 --> 00:48:32.490
That has a misalignment of incentives

1116
00:48:32.490 --> 00:48:34.200
because it's rewarding people

1117
00:48:34.200 --> 00:48:37.470
for that level of judgment and invective.

1118
00:48:37.470 --> 00:48:39.630
We do, but each and every one of us

1119
00:48:39.630 --> 00:48:43.080
can refuse to participate in that and do what we can,

1120
00:48:43.080 --> 00:48:46.140
because there's also some beautiful stuff on social media.

1121
00:48:46.140 --> 00:48:48.697
You know there's a line in the course where it says,

1122
00:48:48.697 --> 00:48:52.140
"Nothing in the world is unholy or holy

1123
00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:55.530
except as determined by its purpose,

1124
00:48:55.530 --> 00:48:57.150
what the mind is doing with it."

1125
00:48:57.150 --> 00:48:58.980
And you can see incredible things,

1126
00:48:58.980 --> 00:49:01.440
credible good that comes from social media,

1127
00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:04.920
and you can see terrible even evil things

1128
00:49:04.920 --> 00:49:06.450
that emerge from social media.

1129
00:49:06.450 --> 00:49:08.040
It all gets back to the ethics

1130
00:49:08.040 --> 00:49:11.555
and the principles of right and wrong

1131
00:49:11.555 --> 00:49:15.420
and enlightenment that I do think so many of us

1132
00:49:15.420 --> 00:49:17.133
are at least trying for.

1133
00:49:18.030 --> 00:49:21.630
A New York Times bestselling author, monk, philanthropist,

1134
00:49:21.630 --> 00:49:25.500
activist and teacher, his holiness, Radhanath Swami

1135
00:49:25.500 --> 00:49:28.470
is a man that radiates love,

1136
00:49:28.470 --> 00:49:31.650
he radiates compassion and grace

1137
00:49:31.650 --> 00:49:34.890
with this really sweet and joyous disposition

1138
00:49:34.890 --> 00:49:38.460
that has immeasurably impacted millions of souls

1139
00:49:38.460 --> 00:49:39.960
across the world.

1140
00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:43.050
In this next clip, Radhanath explains the idea

1141
00:49:43.050 --> 00:49:44.700
of a spiritual calling

1142
00:49:44.700 --> 00:49:47.160
and how following a calling can shape someone

1143
00:49:47.160 --> 00:49:50.013
into the person they were meant to become.

1144
00:49:51.150 --> 00:49:52.990
We all have our calling

1145
00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:57.120
and it's not really so important what age

1146
00:49:57.120 --> 00:49:59.160
we have that calling.

1147
00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:01.530
But when that calling for goodness,

1148
00:50:01.530 --> 00:50:03.090
when that calling for truth,

1149
00:50:03.090 --> 00:50:04.500
when that calling for light,

1150
00:50:04.500 --> 00:50:07.200
when that calling to be the change

1151
00:50:07.200 --> 00:50:09.207
that you want to see in the world.

1152
00:50:09.207 --> 00:50:14.207
And I deeply believe it's God's calling

1153
00:50:14.250 --> 00:50:16.200
coming from within us

1154
00:50:16.200 --> 00:50:20.370
which could echo from around us through other voices too.

1155
00:50:20.370 --> 00:50:23.520
But ultimately it's a calling from within.

1156
00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:27.933
We all have our ways of responding to that calling too,

1157
00:50:28.860 --> 00:50:33.860
but in my life this is how I responded to that calling.

1158
00:50:34.080 --> 00:50:34.913
Hmm.

1159
00:50:35.760 --> 00:50:37.680
Yeah, I suppose in order to heed

1160
00:50:37.680 --> 00:50:40.890
whatever calling is happening,

1161
00:50:40.890 --> 00:50:43.380
you have to be present and aware enough

1162
00:50:43.380 --> 00:50:45.783
to notice it's a rising.

1163
00:50:47.340 --> 00:50:52.340
And sometimes situations in the world that are startling,

1164
00:50:54.240 --> 00:50:55.773
that are worrisome,

1165
00:50:57.420 --> 00:51:00.783
even sometimes situations that appear hopeless,

1166
00:51:03.030 --> 00:51:08.030
they alert us to responding to that calling.

1167
00:51:09.600 --> 00:51:12.510
So you find yourself in London,

1168
00:51:12.510 --> 00:51:14.730
you're trekking about Europe,

1169
00:51:14.730 --> 00:51:16.530
you're in Athens,

1170
00:51:16.530 --> 00:51:19.980
you're sitting on the banks of the Thames

1171
00:51:19.980 --> 00:51:21.510
in London at night,

1172
00:51:21.510 --> 00:51:24.720
soaking in the moon, watching the river flow.

1173
00:51:24.720 --> 00:51:28.680
At some point you have this undeniable urge

1174
00:51:28.680 --> 00:51:31.500
or calling to go to India.

1175
00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:34.470
So walk me through that experience.

1176
00:51:34.470 --> 00:51:38.793
Well, coming from northern Illinois,

1177
00:51:43.080 --> 00:51:46.923
I went to Europe with two of my friends.

1178
00:51:47.940 --> 00:51:51.090
We were supposed to be there for just two months

1179
00:51:51.090 --> 00:51:55.950
and then come back for going back to college.

1180
00:51:55.950 --> 00:51:58.390
I went to one semester of a college

1181
00:51:59.281 --> 00:52:00.510
(Radhanath Swami laughing)

1182
00:52:00.510 --> 00:52:03.783
and that was the plan, that was our plan.

1183
00:52:05.190 --> 00:52:09.150
But we got robbed the first day we were in Europe

1184
00:52:09.150 --> 00:52:11.940
and we had no money,

1185
00:52:11.940 --> 00:52:15.540
and one of my friends went back to America that same day

1186
00:52:15.540 --> 00:52:17.163
and the other-

1187
00:52:18.570 --> 00:52:22.500
We made the scene in various places

1188
00:52:22.500 --> 00:52:24.360
and we were very popular,

1189
00:52:24.360 --> 00:52:29.360
and in many ways we saw there was the world

1190
00:52:30.540 --> 00:52:32.520
was opening up to us like anything,

1191
00:52:32.520 --> 00:52:34.680
even though we really didn't have a home,

1192
00:52:34.680 --> 00:52:35.970
or money or anything

1193
00:52:35.970 --> 00:52:38.700
just because the interactions

1194
00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:41.643
with such beautiful people and such opportunities.

1195
00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:47.940
But this calling, this little whisper

1196
00:52:47.940 --> 00:52:51.690
to find a spiritual connection

1197
00:52:51.690 --> 00:52:54.030
was always there in my heart,

1198
00:52:54.030 --> 00:52:57.570
and it was getting louder and louder and louder,

1199
00:52:57.570 --> 00:53:02.530
and that's really what started me on this quest.

1200
00:53:05.640 --> 00:53:10.020
I was spending a lot of time on riverbanks and in forests

1201
00:53:10.020 --> 00:53:11.820
and I was meditating and reading

1202
00:53:11.820 --> 00:53:16.080
various spiritual literatures from various religions.

1203
00:53:16.080 --> 00:53:17.520
I was going to synagogues,

1204
00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:20.523
I was going to churches, I was going to cathedrals.

1205
00:53:21.420 --> 00:53:24.150
I lived in Catholic monasteries.

1206
00:53:24.150 --> 00:53:26.280
I was studying, I was searching,

1207
00:53:26.280 --> 00:53:28.740
I was going to museums to study art,

1208
00:53:28.740 --> 00:53:31.290
to find spiritual clues.

1209
00:53:31.290 --> 00:53:34.320
And eventually I was living in a cave

1210
00:53:34.320 --> 00:53:38.760
on the Island of Crete, which is part of Greece.

1211
00:53:38.760 --> 00:53:43.110
And I was just praying to God for direction.

1212
00:53:43.110 --> 00:53:48.110
And it was there that I had this voice within my heart

1213
00:53:49.740 --> 00:53:51.840
that said, go to India.

1214
00:53:51.840 --> 00:53:54.780
And I had never met an Indian person in my life,

1215
00:53:54.780 --> 00:53:58.500
I've never ate a chili pepper in my life.

1216
00:53:58.500 --> 00:54:02.490
I had no map, I had no money for a map,

1217
00:54:02.490 --> 00:54:05.280
but I just left my friend the next day

1218
00:54:05.280 --> 00:54:09.624
and started hitch hiking to India.

1219
00:54:09.624 --> 00:54:11.220
(Radhanath Swami and Rich laughing)

1220
00:54:11.220 --> 00:54:14.370
And I just believed if I just go in the eastern direction,

1221
00:54:14.370 --> 00:54:15.300
eventually I'll get there.

1222
00:54:15.300 --> 00:54:18.240
You're gonna get there eventually.

1223
00:54:18.240 --> 00:54:20.820
You travel through Afghanistan,

1224
00:54:20.820 --> 00:54:24.180
you're immersed in extreme poverty in that situation,

1225
00:54:24.180 --> 00:54:25.200
you're in Pakistan,

1226
00:54:25.200 --> 00:54:29.220
you have this extraordinary story about getting caught up

1227
00:54:29.220 --> 00:54:32.340
at the border between Pakistan and India.

1228
00:54:32.340 --> 00:54:34.408
Can you tell that story?

1229
00:54:34.408 --> 00:54:37.150
Well, I hitchhiked through Turkey

1230
00:54:38.100 --> 00:54:39.633
and then Iran,

1231
00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:43.953
and then through Afghanistan and Pakistan.

1232
00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:50.600
The whole trip took over four months.

1233
00:54:50.910 --> 00:54:53.670
Now I get on an airplane from London

1234
00:54:53.670 --> 00:54:57.240
and I do it in nine hours, the same amount of distance,

1235
00:54:57.240 --> 00:54:59.676
but it's not as educational.

1236
00:54:59.676 --> 00:55:01.290
(Radhanath Swami laughs)

1237
00:55:01.290 --> 00:55:03.213
But it's much safer for sure.

1238
00:55:07.260 --> 00:55:12.260
Finally, when I was in the European countries,

1239
00:55:12.420 --> 00:55:17.370
I was studying Christianity and Judaism.

1240
00:55:17.370 --> 00:55:20.310
When I was in the Middle East,

1241
00:55:20.310 --> 00:55:24.630
I was studying Islam from some Islamic scholars

1242
00:55:24.630 --> 00:55:27.780
and very holy people actually.

1243
00:55:27.780 --> 00:55:32.780
And when I finally came to the border of India.

1244
00:55:35.910 --> 00:55:40.240
India and Pakistan have been in conflict politically

1245
00:55:41.430 --> 00:55:43.563
since the partition.

1246
00:55:44.520 --> 00:55:48.333
And there's a no man's land between the two countries.

1247
00:55:49.230 --> 00:55:52.830
And when I left the border of Pakistan,

1248
00:55:52.830 --> 00:55:57.150
I had to walk a couple hours through this no man's land

1249
00:55:57.150 --> 00:55:59.730
to get to India.

1250
00:55:59.730 --> 00:56:03.960
And at that time the, the border post was in a forest

1251
00:56:03.960 --> 00:56:07.773
near the city of Firozpur in Punjab.

1252
00:56:08.760 --> 00:56:10.450
And when I arrived

1253
00:56:13.320 --> 00:56:15.393
they asked how much money I had.

1254
00:56:17.280 --> 00:56:20.880
And as far as I remember, I had 26 cents

1255
00:56:20.880 --> 00:56:23.285
in about four different currencies.

1256
00:56:23.285 --> 00:56:24.399
(Rich laughs)

1257
00:56:24.399 --> 00:56:28.083
And the immigration agent was very, very angry.

1258
00:56:30.130 --> 00:56:33.750
She said that, "We have enough beggars in India,

1259
00:56:33.750 --> 00:56:36.030
we don't want another one.

1260
00:56:36.030 --> 00:56:38.070
Go back to where you came from."

1261
00:56:38.070 --> 00:56:38.903
Mm-hmm.

1262
00:56:39.840 --> 00:56:42.930
And I pleaded with her

1263
00:56:42.930 --> 00:56:46.350
that I just hitchhiked from London

1264
00:56:46.350 --> 00:56:49.980
and I got so many diseases that almost killed me,

1265
00:56:49.980 --> 00:56:51.810
and people tried to kill me,

1266
00:56:51.810 --> 00:56:56.460
and so many difficulties I had to get here

1267
00:56:56.460 --> 00:57:00.210
because I want to learn from your people.

1268
00:57:00.210 --> 00:57:02.580
Please give me a chance.

1269
00:57:02.580 --> 00:57:05.520
And she wouldn't do it.

1270
00:57:05.520 --> 00:57:07.650
So for hours and hours and hours

1271
00:57:07.650 --> 00:57:10.290
I was just sitting under a tree

1272
00:57:10.290 --> 00:57:15.150
and kept coming back and asking and getting rejected.

1273
00:57:15.150 --> 00:57:18.060
But the problem was I couldn't go back to Pakistan either

1274
00:57:18.060 --> 00:57:21.150
'cause I only had a one time visa,

1275
00:57:21.150 --> 00:57:23.070
so I was in this no man's land

1276
00:57:23.070 --> 00:57:25.440
and there were no cell phones in 19-

1277
00:57:25.440 --> 00:57:27.840
This was 1970.

1278
00:57:27.840 --> 00:57:31.343
And there was no phone booths, there was no-

1279
00:57:31.343 --> 00:57:32.760
I was just there.

1280
00:57:34.710 --> 00:57:37.713
And finally, just around the sunset time,

1281
00:57:38.580 --> 00:57:41.220
the guards changed,

1282
00:57:41.220 --> 00:57:44.280
and one man from the Sikh community

1283
00:57:44.280 --> 00:57:48.420
who was a soldier took the post.

1284
00:57:48.420 --> 00:57:52.233
And after the other immigration drove off in a jeep,

1285
00:57:53.472 --> 00:57:57.250
I went to him and this man said to me

1286
00:57:58.890 --> 00:58:03.240
that I have been ordered by my commanding officer

1287
00:58:03.240 --> 00:58:07.803
to reject you unless you show me at least $200.

1288
00:58:12.510 --> 00:58:17.510
At that point, I cried and I really cried

1289
00:58:17.730 --> 00:58:22.730
because I was 19 years old and it was hopeless.

1290
00:58:23.940 --> 00:58:25.653
And I begged.

1291
00:58:26.610 --> 00:58:30.900
I said, I've given up everything of my life

1292
00:58:30.900 --> 00:58:34.263
to learn from your country and to learn for your people.

1293
00:58:35.310 --> 00:58:38.050
Please just give me a chance

1294
00:58:38.940 --> 00:58:41.880
and I promise you that someday

1295
00:58:41.880 --> 00:58:44.703
I'll do something good for the people of India.

1296
00:58:46.290 --> 00:58:48.390
And being an immigration agent,

1297
00:58:48.390 --> 00:58:53.390
he looked into my eyes with such a interrogating glance.

1298
00:58:54.300 --> 00:58:58.233
He was just looking into my mind and into my heart.

1299
00:58:59.130 --> 00:59:01.863
For about a minute, he just stared at me.

1300
00:59:04.110 --> 00:59:05.403
And then he spoke,

1301
00:59:06.750 --> 00:59:11.750
he said, "Sometimes a man must follow his heart

1302
00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:15.423
and even though I have been ordered to reject you,

1303
00:59:16.260 --> 00:59:20.910
I am going to give you the chance that you're crying for."

1304
00:59:20.910 --> 00:59:23.617
And then he stamped my passport and said,

1305
00:59:23.617 --> 00:59:25.200
"Welcome to India."

1306
00:59:25.200 --> 00:59:26.670
I love that, that's beautiful.

1307
00:59:26.670 --> 00:59:29.313
I feel like you need to reunite with him.

1308
00:59:31.526 --> 00:59:34.260
I would like to see you visit him again

1309
00:59:34.260 --> 00:59:36.660
and recount that story to him.

1310
00:59:36.660 --> 00:59:39.000
I wish we could.
Yeah.

1311
00:59:39.000 --> 00:59:40.023
But that was,

